{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And he spoke, etc., saying: Arm yourselves, etc.</b> One must know: (a) Its saying <b>saying</b> — to whom would they say it? (b) Its saying <b>Arm yourselves</b>, etc. — why did he not command in the same order that he said concerning the war against Amalek to Joshua (Exodus 17:9): ״בְּחַר לָנוּ אֲנָשִׁים״? So too here he should have commanded Pinchas, whom he sent with them, and not the entirety of the people. Moreover, this way is not proper, for who would persuade people to know who it is that will rouse himself to go forth, and each one will say, the commandment is not upon me to perform. (c) Its saying <b>and they shall be upon Midian</b> — for he should have said ״and they shall do battle with Midian,״ in the manner of His saying (ibid.) ״הִלָּחֵם בַּעֲמָלֵק״. (d) Why did he change the words of HaShem, for HaShem said to him ״נְקֹם נִקְמַת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, yet he said ״נִקְמַת ה׳״, and he should have been precise with the statement of HaShem. (e) Its saying <b>a thousand per tribe</b> — why did he not send all the people to take vengeance upon the wicked nations, and on what basis did he send a small number of men, twelve thousand, against a great people? Go out and learn their vast numbers from their infants alone who remained, who were thirty-two thousand." "<b>However,</b> according to what we have introduced — that there is in this matter of war a concern of injustice — and therefore Elohim came with the pleasantness of His words to Moses: ״נְקֹם״, etc., to apprise you of the order of battle in the arrangement through which the matter of vengeance that HaShem commanded would be fulfilled. For this reason he acted wisely and did not wish to select men by means of others, for who knows the men who did not scheme evil and did not stumble in the snare of that nation? He counseled and said to them that they should arm themselves from the congregation — meaning, from themselves: each one who knows himself to be pure-hearted and clean-handed should present himself to the count of a thousand per tribe. This is what he alluded to in the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b>: the hidden statement, which is the matter of their cleanliness from the sin of Midian — for each one of those presenting himself to discern within himself whether he is clean and pure. And perhaps he also alluded in the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> to the matter of forbidden relations, as they said, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 56b): ״לֵאמֹר״ — this is the revealing of forbidden relations; for he said to them that those who arm themselves must be clean from sin. And as he finished saying ״אֲנָשִׁים לַצָּבָא״, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Tanchuma): all אֲנָשִׁים are righteous. And even though we have explained in other places that this is only so when the word אֲנָשִׁים is superfluous, in what is before us its fellow will testify to it — namely what we have examined concerning the matter. Therefore they need not fear a stumbling block, since those who go forth to the army are righteous. Furthermore, they will be called pious, since this sin came into their hands and they were saved from it, as they said, of blessed memory (Zohar Chadash 3:155a): therefore one is called a חָסִיד. Through such a medium the vengeance will come about without concern. Therefore he did not wish for all Israel to present themselves to the army, for in such a manner there is a concern of hindering the vengeance — and on the contrary, the opposite, as will be mentioned:" "<b>And they shall be upon Midian</b> — this may be explained based on what the men of truth have said: that the aspect of holiness is the foundation of ascent, and the aspect of the kelipah is the sod of descent. And when a person sins and a portion of evil cleaves to him, it activates in him its nature and its root to lower him. This particular matter we have explained in the portion כִּי תִשָּׂא אֶת רֹאשׁ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל (Exodus 30:12): that the sinner bends his head like an agmon and stands in the category of lowliness. And this is its saying <b>and they shall be upon Midian</b> — meaning, that there should not be in them the impurity that surrounds, which lowers them to Midian, but rather they shall be in the category of the good, which stands above." "And its saying <b>״to execute the vengeance of HaShem״</b> — this too is part of the wisdom to achieve the vengeance: that he commanded them that the intent of the vengeance should be zeal for HaShem, for they caused the nullification of His commandments and His will, blessed be He, and not to take vengeance for what resulted from the sin — that several of Israel died. For when the intent is upon this, there is in the deed no commandment to support their medium to take vengeance from the enemy. And even though HaShem said to him ״נִקְמַת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, He did not say to him that he should say this language to Israel; rather, His words were with Moses: ״נְקֹם נִקְמַת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, and Moses acted wisely to do the matter in this manner — to fulfill the vengeance of Israel that he was commanded concerning by the statement ״נְקֹם״." "<b>Further,</b> the verse may be explained in this manner: that there is no need for them to do battle themselves, but rather they shall be there upon them — meaning, for their sake. And the reason — <b>to execute the vengeance of HaShem</b>: meaning, a vengeance that HaShem will take against Midian through their medium, in the manner of His saying (Exodus 14:14) ״ה׳ יִלָּחֵם לָכֶם״." "And the verse says <b>וַיִּמָּסְרוּ מֵאַלְפֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל</b> — meaning: whoever knew of himself that he stood in his righteousness delivered himself for the matter, until they reached the full count of a thousand. And although there were in Israel priest after priest among the righteous, HaShem acted wisely so as not to shame those who remained, that they not be conspicuous."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:25.004385252Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}